chmod is a Linux command used to change the permissions of files and directories, controlling who can read, write, or execute them.

ls -l displays files and directories in a detailed format, showing their permissions, ownership, size, and modification date.

useradd is a Linux command used to create a new user account on the system.

The chmod command helps protect files by restricting access to authorized users and groups.

The ls -l and useradd commands help administrators inspect file details and manage user accounts effectively.
